WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.650
A country poured all of its money into a single harbour.

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:11.780
Among the goods loaded onto the ships were wigs, combs and woollen stockings; the place they were bound for was equatorial rainforest.

3
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:17.030
Two years later Scotland had neither a colony left nor gold enough to stay independent.

4
00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.520
A country poured all of its money into a single harbour.

5
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:37.810
Among the goods loaded onto the ships were wigs, combs and woollen stockings; the place they were bound for was the rainforest of the equator.

6
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:43.540
Two years later Scotland was left with neither a colony, nor enough gold to remain independent.

7
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:51.190
In sixteen ninety-four, in London, among the names written onto the founding documents of the Bank of England there was a Scot.

8
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:56.920
William Paterson had been born in the countryside of Dumfriesshire, the child of a shepherd family.

9
00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:05.050
In his youth he had gone out to the West Indies, traded in the Caribbean, and sat talking in harbours with pirates, merchants and smugglers.

10
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.180
When he came home he had no money in his pocket, only an idea.

11
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.950
He put that idea first to London, then to Hamburg, then to Amsterdam.

12
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.240
Every one of them listened, and every one of them politely turned him down.

13
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.770
Paterson did not give up.

14
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:26.500
Because as far as he was concerned, what he held was a key that could rebuild the whole of world trade.

15
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.110
The idea was simple, as all dangerous ideas are.

16
00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:37.960
Look at a map of the world: North and South America are joined to one another by a thin strip of land in Panama.

17
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:45.770
There, in the region called the Isthmus of Darién, the distance between the two oceans falls in places to as little as eighty kilometres.

18
00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:52.940
At that time there were two ways of carrying goods from Europe to Asia: rounding the Cape of Good Hope at the southern end of Africa,

19
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:58.670
or passing through the icy waters of the Strait of Magellan at the far tip of South America.

20
00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.640
Both took months, and both devoured ships and men.

21
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.090
Paterson's proposal was this: let a harbour be built at Darién.

22
00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:16.340
Let goods arriving from the Atlantic be unloaded there, carried overland across the isthmus, and loaded on the other shore onto ships putting out into the Pacific.

23
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.630
Whoever held that harbour would take a toll on the trade of the world.

24
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:26.040
In Paterson's own words, this was the door of the seas and the key of the universe.

25
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.530
The idea had something concrete behind it as well.

26
00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:39.580
A surgeon named Lionel Wafer had been wounded while crossing the isthmus with buccaneers in the early sixteen eighties, had been left behind, and had lived for months among the native Guna people.

27
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:48.990
By his account Darién was green, its water plentiful, its soil fertile, its people hated the Spanish, and the Spanish had never truly managed to settle there.

28
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:55.200
In sixteen ninety-seven Paterson's company quietly brought Wafer to Edinburgh and questioned him.

29
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.530
Wafer described the region as a paradise.

30
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.500
Nobody asked him how much rain fell there for six months of the year.

31
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.910
So why did this Caribbean dream find an answer in Scotland rather than in London?

32
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.080
Because in those years Scotland was desperate.

33
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:19.690
Since sixteen oh three it had shared a king with England, but it was a separate country: it had its own parliament, its own law, its own treasury.

34
00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:26.060
Yet the Navigation Acts passed by England had reserved trade with the English colonies to English ships.

35
00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:32.750
Scottish merchants, though subjects of the same king, were shut out of the most profitable markets in America.

36
00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:42.960
On top of that, in the middle of the sixteen nineties the country was struck by one harvest disaster after another; in this famine, remembered in history as the Seven Ill Years,

37
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.850
a significant part of the population in some districts died of hunger or of the diseases that hunger brought.

38
00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:55.860
Scotland had no colony, no navy, little gold, and it watched from the sidelines as its neighbour grew rich.

39
00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:07.510
It was in this atmosphere that, in June of sixteen ninety-five, the Scottish Parliament passed an act and founded the Company of Scotland Trading to Africa and the Indies, the Company of Scotland for short.

40
00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:16.600
The company was given a thirty-one-year monopoly on trade with Africa and Asia, the power to found colonies, and exemption from customs duties.

41
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.730
On paper, Scotland had created its own East India Company.

42
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.060
Half of the capital was to be raised in London.

43
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:32.030
In the first days things went well; within a short time subscriptions worth three hundred thousand pounds were written into the books opened in London.

44
00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:35.520
Then the East India Company saw what was happening.

45
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.050
They had no intention of allowing a venture that would raise a rival to their own monopoly to be financed with English capital.

46
00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:49.380
The English Parliament moved, and threatened to investigate and prosecute the Englishmen who had put money into the Company of Scotland.

47
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:53.030
Within a week the London investors withdrew their signatures.

48
00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:57.320
The company turned to Hamburg; the merchants there seemed interested,

49
00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:05.610
but the king's representative in Hamburg intervened and let it be known that anyone doing business with the Scots would draw the displeasure of the English king.

50
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.460
Hamburg closed as well.

51
00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.830
So did Amsterdam.

52
00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:18.480
And so the Company of Scotland found every door shut, and a single option remained: to raise the money from inside Scotland itself.

53
00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.650
No outside observer thought this was possible.

54
00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:26.260
The country was poor, it had gone hungry, it was short of cash.

55
00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.390
The company's directors opened a book in Edinburgh and began to wait.

56
00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:38.840
When the subscription book opened in Edinburgh in February of sixteen ninety-six, what happened was something without precedent in Scottish history.

57
00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.210
The queue spilled into the street.

58
00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:45.340
Nobles came, lawyers came, merchants came.

59
00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:53.950
But they were not the striking part: weavers, tailors, farmers, widowed women, ministers and small-town shopkeepers came too.

60
00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:59.520
One woman subscribed a hundred pounds, another brought the last money she had put by.

61
00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:06.050
Towns took shares as institutions; Glasgow, Edinburgh and Perth put up money in their own names.

62
00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.260
The guilds opened their coffers.

63
00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:12.230
Church congregations gathered it among themselves.

64
00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.520
The reason for this was not only the expectation of profit.

65
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.330
The undertaking was seen less as an investment than as an answer.

66
00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:28.140
A country humiliated for years, shut out of trade, wrestling with hunger, was going to build an empire by its own strength.

67
00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.630
Every name written into the books was a kind of declaration.

68
00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:36.400
In the end the subscriptions raised reached four hundred thousand pounds.

69
00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.330
What that figure is worth today does not matter; what matters is the proportion.

70
00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:47.380
By the estimates of historians this money was roughly a fifth of the capital in circulation in Scotland.

71
00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:55.990
A country had tied a fifth of its liquid wealth to a single company, a single plan, a single swamp that most of them could not even have pointed to on a map.

72
00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.960
Once the money was raised, the ships came.

73
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:07.450
Scotland had no shipyard able to build such a fleet, so the ships were commissioned or bought in Amsterdam and Hamburg.

74
00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:16.380
Five vessels were made ready: the Caledonia, the Saint Andrew, the Unicorn, and two small tenders, the Dolphin and the Endeavour.

75
00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.310
The goods loaded into the holds gave the earliest warning of how the business would go.

76
00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:32.320
For sale at the great trading centre to be founded at Darién, the company loaded woollen stockings, heavy woollen cloth, wigs, combs, shoes, clay pipes and Bibles.

77
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:39.330
None of this would be of any use to anyone in a forest a few degrees from the equator, where the humidity reached ninety percent.

78
00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:44.260
The local people wanted guns, axes, knives, ironware.

79
00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:47.270
The Scots were taking them wigs.

80
00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.400
Paterson himself had lost his standing by the time he set out.

81
00:07:51.400 --> 00:08:00.010
A man who ran the company's business in London had taken a large sum from the till and vanished, and the responsibility had been laid indirectly on Paterson.

82
00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:08.620
The architect of the whole project was removed from the board of directors and boarded for Darién as an ordinary settler, with no official authority whatsoever.

83
00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:18.350
The colony was to be governed by a council of seven working under a presidency that rotated weekly; there was disagreement over how decisions would be taken before they had even sailed.

84
00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:25.520
In the summer of sixteen ninety-eight, in July, five ships weighed anchor from Leith, the port of Edinburgh.

85
00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:30.290
Thousands had gathered on the quay and on the hills around it.

86
00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:38.100
There were some twelve hundred people aboard: farmers, craftsmen, old soldiers, a few ministers, women and children.

87
00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:42.390
Most of the crews and the passengers did not know where the ships were going.

88
00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:48.120
The orders given to the captains were sealed; they were not to be opened before Madeira was passed.

89
00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.050
The secrecy was meant to keep the Spanish or the English from stopping the expedition.

90
00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.420
The voyage took four months.

91
00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:59.550
The seals were broken and the course was turned towards the Caribbean.

92
00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:08.000
Around seventy people died of illness on the way and were given to the sea; for an ocean crossing of that age this was not an unusual loss.

93
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:15.810
On the second of November, sixteen ninety-eight, the fleet entered a wide, sheltered bay on what is today the north-eastern coast of Panama.

94
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.580
The water was clear, the bay was deep, it was sheltered from the wind.

95
00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:31.110
When the settlers came ashore, the scene before them seemed to confirm what Wafer had described: dense forest, tall trees, running streams, a magnificent natural harbour.

96
00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.640
They named the bay Caledonia Bay.

97
00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:38.890
They called the country they would build New Caledonia, and the town they would build New Edinburgh.

98
00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:48.300
On a narrow peninsula commanding the entrance to the bay they laid the foundations of Fort Saint Andrew, named for Scotland's patron saint; fifty cannon were placed there.

99
00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:54.350
Friendly relations were established with the Guna leaders nearby, gifts were given, agreements were made.

100
00:09:54.350 --> 00:10:00.400
The first letters were sent to Scotland, and those letters were read in Edinburgh with great joy.

101
00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.090
The colony had been founded.

102
00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:07.220
On paper, everything had gone exactly as planned.

103
00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:10.070
Then the rain began.

104
00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:13.560
Darién is one of the wettest regions in the world.

105
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:18.650
For most of the year the rain falls for days, for weeks, without a break.

106
00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:23.420
The ground beneath the settlement they had built turned to mud in a short time.

107
00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:29.310
The ditches they dug filled up, the insides of the tents took water, the roofs of the huts leaked.

108
00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:35.040
The flat ground around the fort turned into a swamp, and in that swamp mosquitoes bred.

109
00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.010
At that time nobody knew that malaria was carried by mosquitoes.

110
00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:44.260
The illness was thought to come from bad air, from the foul vapour rising off the marsh.

111
00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:49.510
The settlers were facing an enemy against which they knew no means of protecting themselves.

112
00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:54.760
Malaria, yellow fever and dysentery spread almost at once.

113
00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:59.210
The people who had come ashore in November took to their beds in January.

114
00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:01.740
The food ran out just as quickly.

115
00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:10.830
The salted meat and ship's biscuit brought in the vessels had spoiled over the four-month voyage and in the tropical damp; mouldy flour and maggoty meat were being handed out.

116
00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:18.800
The settlers tried to plant yams and maize, but they had missed the planting season, and the fields they cleared out of the forest did not hold up in the rain.

117
00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.810
Rations were cut, then cut again.

118
00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:25.140
Weakened bodies gave way to disease more easily.

119
00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.630
The dream of trade stayed in that same mud.

120
00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:35.160
The Guna people were friendly, but they had no capital to enter into trade with, and no need for the goods the Scots had brought.

121
00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.770
Nobody in an equatorial forest bought woollen stockings or wigs.

122
00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:48.220
What had really been expected was that the bay would turn into a great port of call, that ships would come from Europe and from America and unload their goods.

123
00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.430
The ships did not come.

124
00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:57.600
And those that did had no wish to take the risk of trading at a harbour that counted as Spanish waters and that no great power recognised.

125
00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.970
The council did not work.

126
00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:06.660
A presidency rotating weekly among seven men meant that every week a new decision undid the decision of the week before.

127
00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:12.710
It was not clear who was to give orders, discipline collapsed, theft and desertion began.

128
00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:19.240
Paterson, though he carried the whole weight of the project on his shoulders, stood at the edge with no official say at all.

129
00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:24.010
Within a few weeks of their landing, his wife and child fell ill and died.

130
00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:32.300
Paterson caught the fever too and was out of his senses for weeks; when he recovered, those who knew him said he was no longer the man he had been.

131
00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:37.870
By the spring of sixteen ninety-nine, dying had become a daily piece of work.

132
00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:42.640
According to the records, at the worst of it ten people a day were being buried.

133
00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.490
The graveyard was larger than the fort.

134
00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:49.300
Those left alive did not have the strength to carry the dead.

135
00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.990
In June the decision was taken.

136
00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:55.480
The colony would be abandoned.

137
00:12:55.480 --> 00:13:02.490
Those who remained were put aboard the ships, the sick were carried to the bunks, the cannon and the goods were left behind.

138
00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:07.420
In July the fleet left the bay, and New Caledonia stood empty after eight months.

139
00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:11.070
But putting out to sea was not deliverance.

140
00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:18.880
The people on the ships were already sick, and now they were travelling in crowded, airless holds, in vessels whose fresh water had given out.

141
00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:23.010
The number who died on the way came close to the number who had died ashore.

142
00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:27.620
The Saint Andrew lost the greater part of its crew on the way to Jamaica.

143
00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:33.670
The Unicorn barely reached the coast of North America, and was too rotten ever to put to sea again.

144
00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:40.360
When the Caledonia arrived at New York, there were only a few people left with the strength to come up on deck.

145
00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:45.610
Of the twelve hundred people who had set out from Leith, around three hundred were alive.

146
00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:50.540
The rest had been left in the mud of Darién or in the water of the Caribbean.

147
00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.550
And this was only the first half of the story.

148
00:13:53.550 --> 00:14:01.040
Because while they were dying, a second fleet in Scotland, knowing nothing of any of it, was preparing to set out for that same bay.

149
00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.850
It was not illness alone that hastened the collapse of the colony.

150
00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:12.500
The king who sat in London was the king of the Scots as well, but he was at the same time the man who conducted England's foreign policy.

151
00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:20.950
England's greatest anxiety at that moment was not to set Spain against it in the succession struggle that would break out on the death of the childless and ailing Spanish king.

152
00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:25.720
And Darién was a region that Spain reckoned on paper as its own territory.

153
00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:32.250
The Scots building a fort there was seen in Madrid as an outright invasion, and it brought fierce protests.

154
00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.620
The king's solution was plain.

155
00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:48.190
An order was sent to the governors of the English colonies in America, from Jamaica to New York, from Barbados to Boston: no help of any kind was to be given to the Scottish colony, no provisions sold, no arms supplied,

156
00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.200
no harbour opened to its ships.

157
00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:57.090
That order cut away the nearest sources of help from a starving New Caledonia with a stroke of the pen.

158
00:14:57.090 --> 00:15:06.660
Scottish boats that went from Darién to Jamaica to fetch provisions came back without being able to buy a single sack of flour, even though they were able to tie up in the harbour.

159
00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:12.390
The Scots never forgot it: their own king had ordered his own subjects to go hungry.

160
00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.560
Meanwhile help had set out from Scotland.

161
00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:24.010
When two supply ships reached the bay in the summer of sixteen ninety-nine they found the colony empty; the survivors had pulled out a few weeks before.

162
00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:33.580
One of the ships, the Olive Branch, burned away entirely in a fire that broke out in its hold while it lay at anchor in the bay; all the provisions aboard turned to ash.

163
00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:36.110
The real tragedy came after that.

164
00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:44.720
Before the company had yet learned what had happened to the first fleet, at the end of the summer of sixteen ninety-nine it sent out a second convoy of four ships.

165
00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:49.650
The largest among them was the Rising Sun, carrying thirty-eight guns.

166
00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:56.660
There were more than a thousand people in that convoy, and they believed the place they were going to was an established, working colony.

167
00:15:56.660 --> 00:16:07.030
When they entered Caledonia Bay on the thirtieth of November, sixteen ninety-nine, what they saw was empty huts that the forest had begun to take back, a fallen fort and hundreds of graves.

168
00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.880
Turning back was not easy either.

169
00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:17.850
The ships were worn, the people were tired, and to return to Scotland empty-handed meant confessing that they had destroyed all the country's money.

170
00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.220
They decided to stay.

171
00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:25.790
Before long the same cycle began: rain, swamp, fever, death.

172
00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:33.120
Again the leadership could not be shared; the ministers blamed sinfulness, the members of the council blamed one another.

173
00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:37.250
In February of seventeen hundred a brief gleam of hope appeared.

174
00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:45.700
An officer named Alexander Campbell of Fonab, seasoned in the European wars, arrived with a relief ship and effectively took command.

175
00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:56.710
When he learned that the Spanish were preparing to besiege the colony he did not wait; he plunged into the forest with a small force and stormed the Spanish forward post at Toubacanti in a direct assault.

176
00:16:56.710 --> 00:17:01.160
The attack succeeded, and the Spanish left their positions and withdrew.

177
00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:06.570
But Campbell was wounded in the chest, and shortly after his return the fever put him in bed.

178
00:17:06.570 --> 00:17:12.940
The one competent commander the colonists had found was unable to fight within days of the victory he had won.

179
00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:15.950
The Spanish answer was measured and final.

180
00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:24.720
A fleet under the command of Juan Pimienta closed the mouth of the bay, troops were put ashore, and Fort Saint Andrew was besieged from land and from sea.

181
00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:28.530
The Spanish did not storm it; there was no need.

182
00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:30.900
The siege lasted a month.

183
00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:37.430
The Scots' water ran low, their powder ran low, the number of able-bodied men fell every day.

184
00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:42.360
The guns fired, trenches were dug, the sick died inside the fort.

185
00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:45.690
In the end Pimienta offered to talk.

186
00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:51.420
On the thirty-first of March, seventeen hundred, terms of surrender were signed.

187
00:17:51.420 --> 00:18:02.590
The terms were generous, considering the strength of the besieger: the Scots would march out of the fort with their colours, with their arms and with their drums beating, board their ships and leave, never to return.

188
00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:07.040
In April New Caledonia was emptied for the second and last time.

189
00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:15.650
The Spanish walked into the empty fort, and the region went back to being what it had been, a place where no European state ever managed to plant a settlement.

190
00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:19.140
The voyage home struck the last blow.

191
00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:28.710
The Rising Sun, lying at anchor off Charleston on the coast of North America for repairs, was caught in a hurricane in September of seventeen hundred and went down.

192
00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:32.680
Of the more than a hundred people aboard, not one survived.

193
00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:44.650
The only name to come through was that of a young minister, Archibald Stobo, who had happened to go ashore that day to preach; he stayed in America and died years later as a man who founded churches in South Carolina.

194
00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:49.900
Some two thousand five hundred people in all had taken part in the two expeditions.

195
00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:53.710
Those who made it back to Scotland numbered a few hundred.

196
00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:59.280
The news reached Edinburgh drop by drop, and every drop was worse than the one before it.

197
00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:02.290
The loss was not only in people.

198
00:19:02.290 --> 00:19:06.260
A fifth of the country's cash capital had evaporated.

199
00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:13.110
Widows who had bought shares lost their livelihoods, the guilds found their coffers emptied, town governments went into debt.

200
00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:20.600
For a generation in Scotland, having put money into Darién was used as the explanation for a family's fall into poverty.

201
00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:23.770
The anger was turned first on the king.

202
00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:29.660
The ban on assistance sent to the colonies was, for the Scots, the proof of betrayal.

203
00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:32.190
Then it turned on England.

204
00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:43.200
In seventeen oh five the captain of an English merchant ship called the Worcester, detained off Leith, and two of his men were tried on the charge of having killed the crew of a missing Scottish ship.

205
00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:50.530
The evidence was extremely weak, but the streets of Edinburgh wanted blood, and the three men were hanged on the sands of Leith.

206
00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:54.820
This showed the point the relationship between the two countries had reached.

207
00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:57.670
England's response was economic.

208
00:19:57.670 --> 00:20:07.400
With the measure it passed in seventeen oh five, known as the Alien Act, the parliament in London announced that if Scotland did not sit down to union talks by a certain date,

209
00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:14.250
it would count Scots as aliens in England and forbid the entry of Scottish cattle, coal and linen into England.

210
00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:20.460
For a country whose economy was already on its knees after Darién, this was a pressure that ended the argument.

211
00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:27.630
The talks began in seventeen oh six, and in seventeen oh seven the two parliaments ratified the treaty of union.

212
00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:35.440
Scotland closed its own parliament; Scotland and England became a single kingdom, a single customs area, a single parliament.

213
00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:40.210
Among the articles of the treaty stood one of the most striking items in history.

214
00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:49.940
Article fifteen provided for a payment to Scotland of three hundred and ninety-eight thousand and eighty-five pounds and ten shillings; in the documents the name of this money was the Equivalent.

215
00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:56.470
Its official justification was that it was compensation for Scotland taking on part of England's national debt.

216
00:20:56.470 --> 00:21:07.320
Its actual use was something else: the greater part of this money went to repaying the shareholders of the Company of Scotland the capital they had put in, together with five percent interest.

217
00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:13.690
Everyone who had lost money at Darién got that money back on the day Scotland gave up its independent parliament.

218
00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:18.300
How this payment is to be read has been argued over for three centuries.

219
00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:25.630
On one view the Equivalent was an honest piece of accounting; an account was being closed as Scotland entered a new financial union.

220
00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:34.720
On another view it was the purchase of a nation's sovereignty, and the people who put the money in their pockets were some of the same people as the members of parliament who voted.

221
00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:45.890
Robert Burns, some eighty years later, cast this idea of a purchase into lines in his poem "Such a Parcel of Rogues in a Nation", saying that English gold had taken the place of Scottish gold,

222
00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:49.060
and those lines are still sung today.

223
00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:52.070
The Equivalent had a sequel of its own.

224
00:21:52.070 --> 00:22:03.080
The company set up to manage the payment, carry out the distribution and pursue the remaining claims received a royal charter in seventeen twenty-seven and became the Royal Bank of Scotland.

225
00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:09.770
Which is to say, the liquidation desk of the Darién disaster is the place where one of Scotland's largest banks was born.

226
00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:18.060
Paterson, for his part, wore out the doors of government for years and was in the end paid compensation of more than eighteen thousand pounds.

227
00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:26.670
He died in seventeen nineteen, having seen the bank he founded grow powerful in London and the colony he had designed wiped off the map.

228
00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:29.520
The idea itself was not wrong.

229
00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:32.210
What was wrong was the place and the time.

230
00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:43.220
Paterson's notion of joining the two oceans came to pass two hundred years after him, some two hundred kilometres west along the same isthmus, with modern engineering and the resources of a great state;

231
00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:50.390
when the Panama Canal opened in nineteen fourteen, world trade was reshaped in exactly the way Paterson had foreseen.

232
00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:54.680
Darién itself, by contrast, was never tamed.

233
00:22:54.680 --> 00:23:05.050
Today the one broken link in the highway that runs the length of North and South America is there; that strip of forest and swamp of roughly a hundred kilometres, known on maps as the Darién Gap,

234
00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:08.540
still has no road laid through it three hundred years later.

235
00:23:08.540 --> 00:23:13.790
The real lesson that remains is something colder than anything to do with the climate or the mosquitoes.

236
00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:19.840
Darién showed that a small country cannot step in among the great trading empires without their permission.

237
00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:30.050
The colony did not die of disease alone; it died of having the ports it could trade with closed to it, of being unable to raise credit, of being under the protection of no navy at all.

238
00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:37.540
And when a nation piles its whole risk onto a single card, what it loses when that card turns is not only money.

239
00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:42.310
The money Scotland lost at Darién was repaid in seventeen oh seven.

240
00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:46.440
The parliament it lost did not come back until nineteen ninety-nine.

241
00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:50.890
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

242
00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:54.220
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
